<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berthold IV of Zähringen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1125–<a href="/years/1186">1186</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Berthold IV, Duke of Zähringen · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berthold IV of Zähringen was Duke of Zähringen from 1152 until his death in 1186 and rector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, the imperial office that gave his family authority west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. He strengthened Zähringen power in the Swiss plateau by founding towns, above all Fribourg in 1157 on a defensible bend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarine">Saane</a>. His son Berthold V, the last of the line, founded Bern.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berthold-iv-of-zahringen.jpg" alt="Berthold IV of Zähringen" width="320" height="530" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flominator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berthold_IV._closeup.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Berthold was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-duke-of-zahringen">Conrad I, Duke of Zähringen</a>, and Clementia of Luxembourg-Namur, and succeeded his father in 1152. He also claimed the title of Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, which he had agreed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> to receive after the two had conquered the Cisjuran, or French, lands of Burgundy together, and after helping him in his Italian campaign of 1152.</p><p>The conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> failed, and in 1156 Barbarossa instead married Beatrice, daughter of the last Count of Burgundy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anscarids">House of Ivrea</a>, which undercut Berthold's claims. In return Berthold was given the title of Rector of Burgundy and made overlord of the Transjuran, or Swiss, parts of Burgundy, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> and Sion. His rivalry with Duke Frederick IV of neighbouring Swabia led him to fight on the side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/welf-vi">Welf VI</a> in the Feud of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> (1164–1166), and in 1173 he became overlord of Zurich.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Berthold died on 8 December 1186. The necrology of the Abbey of Saint Peter in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> records that he gave five candles for the memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>. He was married to Heilwig of Frohburg, and they had three children.</p><p>His son Berthold V was the last Duke of Zähringen, after whom the counts of Kyburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-urach">Urach</a> inherited the family's possessions. A daughter, Agnes, married Count Egino IV of Urach, though the necrology of Tennenbach Abbey names her as the daughter of Berthold V. Another daughter, Anna, married Count Ulrich III of Kyburg; they were the maternal grandparents of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf I of Germany</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Berthold IV of Zähringen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Berthold IV of Zähringen</b> founded Fribourg in 1157 on a rocky bend of the Saane.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-fribourg">Canton of Fribourg</a></li></ul></section>
